## Why This Is Asked
This question explores designing a runtime-extensible policy engine supporting hot-pluggable evaluators, multi-tenancy, and auditable decisions—realistic requirements for large-scale data platforms.

## Key Concepts
- Runtime registries and factories for pluggable evaluators
- Policy pipelines with per-tenant and per-resource scoping
- Chain of Responsibility pattern for sequential evaluation with short-circuiting
- Decision caching with invalidation on policy changes
- Precedence rules: explicit Deny overrides Allow; evaluators run in registered order unless overridden per resource

## Code Example
```typescript
interface EvaluationContext {
  tenant: string
  user: string
  resource: string
  action: string
  headers?: Record<string, string>
}

type Decision = 'Allow' | 'Deny'

interface EvaluationResult {
  decision: Decision
  evaluator: string
  reason?: string
}

interface IPolicyEvaluator {
  name: string
  evaluate(ctx: EvaluationContext): Promise<EvaluationResult>
}

class PolicyRegistry {
  private evaluators = new Map<string, IPolicyEvaluator>()

  register(evaluator: IPolicyEvaluator): void {
    this.evaluators.set(evaluator.name, evaluator)
  }

  get(name: string): IPolicyEvaluator | undefined {
    return this.evaluators.get(name)
  }

  getAll(): IPolicyEvaluator[] {
    return Array.from(this.evaluators.values())
  }
}

class PolicyPipeline {
  constructor(
    private registry: PolicyRegistry,
    private pipelineConfig: Map<string, string[]>
  ) {}

  async evaluate(ctx: EvaluationContext): Promise<EvaluationResult> {
    const evaluatorNames = this.pipelineConfig.get(ctx.resource) 
      ?? this.pipelineConfig.get('default') 
      ?? ['TenantRule', 'IPAllowlist', 'TimeWindow', 'DeviceTrust']

    for (const name of evaluatorNames) {
      const evaluator = this.registry.get(name)
      if (!evaluator) continue

      const result = await evaluator.evaluate(ctx)
      if (result.decision === 'Deny') {
        return result
      }
    }

    return { decision: 'Allow', evaluator: 'pipeline' }
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new PolicyRegistry()
registry.register({ name: 'TenantRule', evaluate: async (ctx) => 
  ctx.tenant ? { decision: 'Allow', evaluator: 'TenantRule' } 
             : { decision: 'Deny', evaluator: 'TenantRule', reason: 'Missing tenant' }
})

const pipeline = new PolicyPipeline(registry, new Map([['default', ['TenantRule']]]))
const result = await pipeline.evaluate({ tenant: 'acme', user: 'alice', resource: 'file', action: 'read' })
```

